<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federal War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Long War (Venezuela), Five Years' War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Federal War was a civil war in Venezuela from 1859 to 1863 between the Conservatives and the Liberals, who demanded a federal system. Federalist leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezequiel-zamora">Ezequiel Zamora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-crisostomo-falcon">Juan Crisóstomo Falcón</a> fought a largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war in which many thousands died. It ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-coche">Treaty of Coche</a> in 1863 and a federal constitution in 1864.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federal-war.jpg" alt="Federal War" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Combate_de_Maiquetia,_el_2_de_septiembre_de_1859.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war arose from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservative-party-uk">Conservative Party</a>'s hold on government posts and land ownership and its refusal of reform, which drove the Liberals, known as the Federalists, to seek greater autonomy for the provinces. Social problems inherited from the struggle for independence, tensions among economic and political groups and a succession of armed risings in the countryside added to the strain. The immediate contest was for power between the conservative government of President Julián Castro, who had ousted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tadeo-monagas">José Tadeo Monagas</a> in March 1858, and the liberal opposition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezequiel-zamora">Ezequiel Zamora</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 20 February 1859 Lieutenant Colonel Tirso Salaverría occupied the military base at Coro and proclaimed a federation, the abolition of the death penalty, universal suffrage and political pluralism, and fighting broke out across the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> won the first major battle, at Santa Inés on 10 December 1859, and secured the Llanos, but he was killed by a sniper on 10 January 1860 while besieging San Carlos, and the siege was abandoned.</p><p>His brother-in-law, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-crisostomo-falcon">Juan Crisóstomo Falcón</a>, took command. On 17 February 1860 government troops under General León de Febres Cordero defeated the Federalists at Coplé, though they withdrew in good order. Falcón turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare and sought help in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, returning in July 1861. Peace talks in December 1861 failed, and in 1862 the Federalists won at Pureche, El Corubo, Mapararí and Buchivacoa. Weakened by thousands of desertions, the government agreed to negotiate after the Federalists surrounded Coro in April 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 22 May 1863 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a> and Falcón signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-coche">Treaty of Coche</a>, named after an estate near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, and Falcón became president on 15 June 1863. Around a hundred thousand people had died in the fighting or of hunger and disease, in a country of just over a million, making it the bloodiest civil war in Venezuela since independence. The death penalty was abolished and the central government's army reduced, while fires had cut farming in the plains and foreign trade declined.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Federal War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Revolution of the Reforms of 1835 briefly overthrew the civilian president José María Vargas, and the <b>Federal War</b> of 1859–1863, fought between Liberal federalists and Conservative centralists, was the bloodiest civil conflict of the century; the Liberal leader Ezequiel Zamora was killed at San Carlos in 1860,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…1824; the Federal Republic of Central America broke apart by the end of the 1830s; Argentina wavered between federal and unitary rule before its federal constitution of 1853; Venezuela became a federation after the <b>Federal War</b> of 1859–1863 and Brazil after the fall of its monarchy in 1889.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
